### Step 1: Calculate the initial and final areas of the loop.

Given:
• Radius of circular loop, \(r = 7 \text{ cm} = 7 \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}\)
• Magnetic field, \(B = 0.2 \text{ T}\)
• Time taken for conversion, \(dt = 0.5 \text{ s}\)
\text{Area of circular loop } A_{1} = \pi r^2 = \pi (7 \times 10^{-2})^2 = 49\pi \times 10^{-4} \text{ m}^2
The circumference of the circular loop is \(2\pi r = 2\pi (7 \times 10^{-2}) = 14\pi \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}\).
When the circular loop is converted into a square loop, its perimeter remains the same.
\text{Perimeter of square loop } = 14\pi \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}
\text{Side length of square loop } a = \frac{14\pi \times 10^{-2}}{4} = \frac{7\pi}{2} \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}
\text{Area of square loop } A_{2} = a^2 = \left(\frac{7\pi}{2} \times 10^{-2}\right)^2 = \frac{49\pi^2}{4} \times 10^{-4} \text{ m}^2

### Step 2: Calculate the change in magnetic flux (\(\Delta\phi\)).

The magnetic field is perpendicular to the plane of the loop, so the angle between the magnetic field and the area vector is 0 degrees. The magnetic flux is given by \(\phi = BA\).
The change in magnetic flux is \(\Delta\phi = B(A_{1} - A_{2})\). (Following the calculation order in the provided solution)
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(49\pi - \frac{49\pi^2}{4}\right) \times 10^{-4}
Using \(\pi \approx 3.14\):
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(49 \times 3.14 - \frac{49 \times (3.14)^2}{4}\right) \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(153.86 - \frac{49 \times 9.8596}{4}\right) \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \left(153.86 - 120.7801\right) \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi = 0.2 \times 33.0799 \times 10^{-4}
\Delta\phi \approx 6.616 \times 10^{-4} \text{ Wb}

### Step 3: Calculate the induced EMF.

The induced EMF is given by Faraday's law of induction:
\text{Induced EMF } |\varepsilon| = \left|\frac{d\phi}{dt}\right|
|\varepsilon| = \frac{6.616 \times 10^{-4}}{0.5} \text{ V}
|\varepsilon| = 13.232 \times 10^{-4} \text{ V}
Converting to milliVolts (mV):
|\varepsilon| = 13.232 \times 10^{-4} \times 10^3 \text{ mV}
|\varepsilon| = 1.3232 \text{ mV}
Rounding to two decimal places, the induced EMF is 1.32 mV.
The final answer is 1.32 mV.

